# Quality Management — Planning
#
# Process Groups: A Practice Guide, 8.1 Plan Quality Management.
#
# Acceptance criteria say what a deliverable must do, and they live on the Scope
# screen. The definition of done says when a card stops moving, and it lives on
# the Board. Neither says how well the work has to be built, or how anybody
# would demonstrate it. That is what this screen is for.

screen: quality
title: Quality Management
phase: Planning
process: 8.1 Plan Quality Management
order: 45
requires: scope
teaches: |
  The difference between "it does what we said" and "it is built well enough",
  and why the second one has to be written down before the work starts. By the
  end you should be able to say what a quality standard is, how it differs from
  an acceptance criterion, and why quality is planned rather than inspected in
  at the end.
before: |
  The scope statement, because a standard is a standard for something. You
  cannot say how well the work has to be done before you have said what the
  work is.
after: |
  The board and the definition of done, which are where a standard stops being
  a statement and starts being something a piece of work is held to.
intro: |
  Quality is the cheapest thing to plan and the most expensive thing to retrofit.
  Three ideas separate here and they get confused constantly.

  An acceptance criterion is what the deliverable must do. A quality standard is
  how well it must be built. A definition of done is when one piece of work
  stops moving. The first is the sponsor's, the second is the team's, and the
  third is the board's.
source: |
  Process Groups: A Practice Guide (PMI, 2022), process 8.1 Plan Quality
  Management, in the Planning process group. Its output is the quality
  management plan: the standards the project will be held to, the metrics that
  demonstrate compliance, and the activities that check them.

  8.2 Manage Quality and 8.3 Control Quality are the doing and the checking.
  This screen is the planning, because a standard agreed after the work is an
  opinion about somebody else's finished deliverable.

field standard
  prompt: What standard does the work have to meet, beyond somebody accepting it?
  check words>=10: Name the standard and how it is checked. "High quality" is a wish; "builds clean at /W4 with no new warnings" is a standard.
  label: Quality standard
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    8.1 Plan Quality Management, output: the quality management plan, which
    states the standards the project will be held to and how compliance is
    demonstrated.
  why: |
    Acceptance criteria say what the deliverable must do. A quality standard
    says how well, and the difference is what stops "it works" and "it is
    finished" from being treated as the same claim.

    The test of a good one is whether two people could disagree about it. If
    they could, it is not a standard yet.
  if_blank: |
    Quality is whatever the person accepting it happened to notice, which means
    it varies with how tired they were.
  example: |
    Builds clean at /W4. Core changes ship with tests. Every field's help panel
    written before the field is called done.

field metrics
  prompt: What gets measured to show the standard is being met, and what is the threshold?
  check rows>=2: One metric is an anecdote. Name at least two, each with a number somebody could check.
  check column:metric: Every row needs the thing being measured.
  check column:threshold: A metric with no threshold cannot be passed or failed. Say what the number has to be.
  label: Quality metrics
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: metric
  tone_column: state
  source: |
    8.1 Plan Quality Management, output: the quality metrics, which describe
    what is measured and how 8.3 Control Quality measures it.
  why: |
    A standard with no metric is a sentence nobody can be held to. The metric is
    what turns it into something that can be passed or failed on a given day.

    Keep the list short. A project measuring fifteen things is measuring none of
    them, because nobody looks at fifteen numbers.
  if_blank: |
    The standard above cannot be demonstrated, so it is enforced by whoever
    remembers it and only when they remember it.
  example: |
    Q1 | Compiler warnings on the core | 0 new at /W4 | Every build | Met
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column metric
    label: What is measured
    type: text
    width: 4
  column threshold
    label: Threshold
    type: line
    width: 2
  column checked
    label: How often
    type: line
    width: 2
  column state
    label: State
    type: choice
    options: Met=ok, At risk=warn, Not met=danger, Not measured yet=faint
    width: 2

field assurance
  prompt: Who checks that the standard is actually being followed, and when?
  check filled: A standard nobody audits is a standard nobody follows. Say who looks and at what point.
  label: How compliance is checked
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    8.2 Manage Quality: the assurance half, which audits whether the processes
    being used will produce work that meets the standard, as distinct from 8.3
    Control Quality, which inspects the deliverables themselves.
  why: |
    Assurance looks at the way the work is done; control looks at what came out.
    Both are needed and people conflate them: inspecting every deliverable while
    never asking whether the process guarantees anything is how teams find the
    same defect twenty times.

    On a small project this is one sentence and one person. Write it anyway,
    because the alternative is that it is nobody.
  if_blank: |
    Nothing connects the standard to the work. It is stated once and then held
    to only when somebody happens to care.
  example: |
    CI runs the core tests and the icon check on every push. The sponsor reads
    the help panel of any field that changed before calling it done.
